WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.380
A transceiver sits at the very edge of the board,

2
00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:06.140
where miswiring and static strike first

3
00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:10.560
SKU-5 puts two bus links on one die,

4
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.042
built for that edge.

5
00:00:12.062 --> 00:00:14.362
The core logic is only a thin skin.

6
00:00:14.842 --> 00:00:20.142
Everything that decides how the part behaves on the wire is analog,

7
00:00:20.142 --> 00:00:22.493
on thick-oxide devices.

8
00:00:22.533 --> 00:00:26.953
How far a signal travels is set by how its edges rise

9
00:00:26.953 --> 00:00:27.613
and fall.

10
00:00:28.833 --> 00:00:32.673
So every edge is shaped, and every delay accounted for.

11
00:00:32.773 --> 00:00:36.973
The outputs drive a half-duplex bus with up to 256 nodes.

12
00:00:37.793 --> 00:00:43.773
Fast over a short run, or slow across 1.2 kilometres: two operating

13
00:00:43.773 --> 00:00:45.655
points, not one.

14
00:00:45.755 --> 00:00:48.215
On the way in, the receiver adds hysteresis.

15
00:00:49.175 --> 00:00:52.555
If the bus is dead or open, the logic sees a defined

16
00:00:52.555 --> 00:00:55.770
state, not noise it could mistake for data.

17
00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:59.830
The second channel does the same job, for CAN-FD.

18
00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:04.050
It has its own supply and its own clock recovery,

19
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.276
sitting right beside it.

20
00:01:06.296 --> 00:01:09.076
Each pin that leaves the board brings its own protection.

21
00:01:10.036 --> 00:01:12.840
A short, or overheating, shuts its channel down.

22
00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.080
SKU-5 is pre-silicon.

23
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:17.860
Every figure is a target.

24
00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:20.440
ESD survival has no silicon data yet

25
00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:22.780
Bring us your cable.

26
00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:23.900
Your node count.

27
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.040
And your fault cases.

28
00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.600
We will tell you what SKU-5 must prove.
